 Lochhead: Compared to what they&#x27;re on the world there. Right, I&#x27;m going to set myself up, right?</p><p>Bob Duncan: I would encourage you to do it. Yeah. Um, because there&#x27;s a lot of good stuff that&#x27;s in there and it&#x27;s really, I think it&#x27;s really intuitive from a customer perspective. And I think if you get using it now and get familiar with it, you&#x27;ll actually start to reap the benefits quite quickly. What I would.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: Say is that something that could be incorporated into it to be fair as well would be credit reports. That would be a good one.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Uh well, Well, it&#x27;s going to be funny you mention that, Karen. So the.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: I&#x27;m actually so forward thinking in life, it&#x27;s unbelievable.</p><p>Bob Duncan: So they are working with a I, don&#x27;t know how they&#x27;re quite going to do it, But they&#x27;re working out with a way where a client will accept an opening banking accept within the platform system. And, then what that will do is it will then pre populate all of the bank account statements straight into my into platform.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: Incorporates all the banking statements.</p><p>Bob Duncan: So with with with open banking, it creates it basically links all your bank accounts together. Yeah. Right. It&#x27;s what banks use at the moment to share data as a as a customer, you have to opt into it. So if you opted into it within platform, you&#x27;re then giving platform access every bank account you have savings account, Current account credit card and then the ability to add the bank statements straight into the system.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: But we they will be doing that if a client uploads themselves anyway. But, the option with the open banking is that it pulls through if client just says, yes, to everything. Correct, yep,</p><p>Bob Duncan: Which means there&#x27;s none of this chasing for bank statements.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: A lot of people might like that but a lot of people will.</p><p>Bob Duncan: But this is it, it&#x27;s not for everybody yeahum but actually from a simplicity perspective, it can save a lot of time and hassle. Okay okay.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: And, Then the client is paying for their advice really as such, and they get the right advice with their mortgage and protection basically. Correct,</p><p>Bob Duncan: Yeah, absolutely spot on. And, then if you think about what lenders are doing from, you know, an income verification perspective, Which is to use HMRC data to verify tax and national insurance payments based on the individual and payslips, Then we&#x27;re going to get to a point hopefully, while whilst we&#x27;re still asking for payslips. Most cases will probably fall under auto income verification, Because they want to get to the point where they&#x27;re using the data to answer the questions. Yeah.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: And they also want to control what we all do, by the way. So. No,</p><p>Bob Duncan: They&#x27;re just trying to make it simple. Stop being up weirdo and thinking that people want to control you.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: No, they do because they don&#x27;t want you getting cash in hand for anything either, like that.</p><p>Bob Duncan: You&#x27;ve got a mobile phone, they&#x27;re already in control of you. You&#x27;ve got a bank account, they&#x27;re already in control of you. So you know, But you&#x27;re a bit of a hypocrite to say that they&#x27;re controlling you when you&#x27;re already part of the system.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: I don&#x27;t get cash in hand for any job I do anyway, so it&#x27;s all the same.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Right, anyway we have gone slightly over. Um back to the spreadsheet for a second. So what I&#x27;ll do then is we&#x27;re going to work onum increasing probably not by many actually mortgage applications for you going from 63 to 70. Keep your standard conversion on insurance, which is currently sitting at about forty-five percent. Ideally, I would say try to work on admin fees. You are currently averaging thirty nine percent. If you can aim to get to fifty, that will then take you. From doing an extra seven mortgage applications purchase cases, your remortgages will be fifty eight, which is sixty percent of what we&#x27;re seeing at the moment. And of that, 60% or 50% are full remortgages, 50% are PTs. So we&#x27;re accounting for that in the costs.</p><p>Bob Duncan: And, then that would mean that we get to banking of about one nine seven for the year, Which would give you a forty k uplift from where you were from last year. So small changes but have the potential to change your output. But you&#x27;re absolutely right, liters is the key one to drive that. And that is what was set for Alan, which Alan will deliver to everybody at the quarterly meeting.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: I Am going to be vocal without being cheeky though,</p><p>Bob Duncan: To be fair, right? Well I have said for the whole year that everybody needs to be much more vocal about it, because Alan is accepting that nobody&#x27;s moaning about it. It&#x27;s not an issue. And that&#x27;s his words.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: It definitely is an issue, right? It is an issue but this isn&#x27;t the conversation for now, right? Because we need to be constructive, right? Because, we&#x27;ve I&#x27;ve been here four years and I&#x27;ve been told the same thing for four years. I was told that they were getting all of this done, I haven&#x27;t seen a change. We&#x27;ve done this through pure grit, the lot of us, not just me, right? Have made this work the way it was you included. But, we&#x27;re constantly being asked and being told that it&#x27;s us where the fault when we&#x27;re not the fault. We want to make money like there&#x27;s nobody wants to make wages better than me right? And Megan and everybody right? But the minute something goes wrong, it&#x27;s our fault and I&#x27;m fed up of it. But that&#x27;s not based on you by the way, it&#x27;s based on.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: The bigger picture.</p><p>Bob Duncan: I know, that&#x27;s what everybody needs to feedback. Yeah,</p><p>Karen Lochhead: Yeah, but everybody does.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Well, This is a thing, you know, it&#x27;s it&#x27;s easy to say you will, and then nobody does it. And then Alan&#x27;s like oh, but Lisa fine, nobody&#x27;s complained about it. But you&#x27;re not right in our business right well can only tell you guys anyway, I will put that onto a what I&#x27;m doing for everyone and what everyone will get. And this then forms part of our new Monday meeting. So. When we come to the Monday meetings, it&#x27;s like, well, what are we aiming to deliver this week? So if you then think about what you&#x27;ve got on purchase cases. So it&#x27;s seventy purchase casesum for the year. If you break that down overum forty.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: So this is what I have to do is going forward. Yeah,</p><p>Bob Duncan: This is what we want you to do. We want you to own this target. So seventy purchase cases, which is one point five purchase cases, a week. Yeah. So one and a half mortgage. Brand new purchase cases, your remortgages are your remortgages. You can&#x27;t control when they drop in, yeah? But the purchase cases is what we want everybody to commit to, to deliver the one and a half a week, which well yours is one and a half or 1.52 cases a week. And, if you deliver the purchase side of it and you do the remortgage stuff, and you maintain your insurance conversion rate and insurance premium, then in theory, you will deliver what you want to deliver. Yeah?</p><p>Bob Duncan: It&#x27;ll only be if one of them changes, like you know, if you don&#x27;t get enough leads, your purchases are flat and all they&#x27;re the same as they were last year. You&#x27;re not going to get to that target. If you remortgage can They&#x27;re a hundred percent flat now.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: They&#x27;re a hundred percent flat now.</p><p>Bob Duncan: No, they are. They are. I mean, I&#x27;m not even I mean I know the leads are down in. Um you know, I&#x27;ve got plenty of data to support that year on year. Um let me just see if I can here we go.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: And like can I just tell you something and you&#x27;re not to repeat this by the way, right? Siobhan is brilliant and she wants to support me, but there&#x27;s a lot of negativity in the branch.</p><p>Bob Duncan: I don&#x27;t know why. I think everybody&#x27;s not from Siobhan,</p><p>Karen Lochhead: Not from Siobhan. She feels it too, right?</p><p>Bob Duncan: I think there&#x27;s a lot of i think it&#x27;s it&#x27;s that time of year that&#x27;s budget planning. Yeah, Yeah, we&#x27;re about three hundred and thirty three leads year to date down on where we were last year. We&#x27;ve still got another month to go so that number will probably drop. Um, you know it&#x27;s likely to be probably a hundred leads down, but we are down. Um, I&#x27;m just going to share my screen finally on this one here, because this just does a year- on- year comparison. So.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: Are you sending me what I need to do though? Will you send that to me?</p><p>Bob Duncan: Yeah, I will complete it. I will fill it in. What we&#x27;re saying is I will budget. I&#x27;ll basically create your plan for the year that says Q 1, I need to I need to write X amount of business, but to do that I need to do X number of applications. Yeah, Q two I need to write X. That&#x27;s going to equal X number of applications. Now whilst I said one and a half applications a week, bear in mind two holidays. Well well no because I&#x27;ve calculated over forty six weeks. Yeah so or sorry over forty eight weeks, Bear in mind that other periods of the year are busier. Other periods are quiet. Yeah. So if you think December naturally is going to be quite quiet.</p><p>Bob Duncan: There will be less business to write in December than there is in the spring market or in the summer market. Yeah, So there might be two in the busy periods and then one later on a year, if that makes sense. But I&#x27;ll do the maths to work it all out. So you have an idea of what you then need to deliver. So when you&#x27;re coming to the week.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: So was it two a week?</p><p>Bob Duncan: No, So if you think about let me show you this calendar, just so I can take this as an example. So.</p><p>Bob Duncan: So if we just focus on purchase cases, so you can see that we&#x27;ve got peaks and troughs, which is the busy periods. So if we say one and a half a week, then that number would be flat. But, that&#x27;s not how the market operates because it&#x27;s busier here and it drops and it gets busy again. And then it drops and it gets busy again. So during the periods of for example February, March and April you&#x27;re going to have to write more than you would write in November, December and January because those are predominantly quiet. Yeah, your May, June and July would be busier than potentially your August and September October. They might actually be about the same. Yeah. So one and a half is the average, but once we break it into the quarters, It might be that you&#x27;ve got some quarters are busier, because it&#x27;s just naturally busier. You know, I&#x27;m not going to be expecting you to write the same level of business in December, January and February compared to on, you know, a September, an April or a May. Yeah? That makes sense? Yeah?</p><p>Bob Duncan: Yeah, so I&#x27;ll work it out and I&#x27;ll say, right, This what we need to write business wise Q one month, one Q two month and so on. And then you&#x27;ll have an idea of what you need to deliver. Yeah. And then that way when you come into the meetings, you can be everybody can be focused to go right. Well, I need to write X This what that looks like. I&#x27;m going to be delivering one and a half cases, That&#x27;s what I&#x27;m aiming for. I&#x27;ve got two purchase cases lined up. I&#x27;ve got a remortgage Right well actually You&#x27;re on track Karen That&#x27;s perfect Yeah.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: I&#x27;m going to do more about getting my own business as well. I&#x27;ve kind of stopped because you&#x27;re dealing with leads, and we&#x27;ve we&#x27;re top heavy with admin. If all that changes, I&#x27;m going to set myself a goal that I&#x27;m not waiting on anybody. I&#x27;m going to start putting myself out there on LinkedIn and stuff like that.</p><p>Bob Duncan: Good, good. Well, You do thatum and that would be that would be amazing. Yeah.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: So I am because I&#x27;m just not waiting for people anymore, but in saying that right, if if they get the leads from the West End, they could potentially be better leads. But all the young ones that I know, right? They should be getting me leads.</p><p>Bob Duncan: They should be. They should be. We&#x27;re talking to the West End, so we&#x27;re in the main bar of us in the Easter break. When do you want to do your first Saturday in the West End?</p><p>Karen Lochhead: Um, hang on, Easter break, right? So what what month have we?</p><p>Bob Duncan: We&#x27;re in April, the ninth. Um,</p><p>Karen Lochhead: I probably would football on the four twenty fourth and go to the doctor on the twenty fourth Right That&#x27;s,</p><p>Bob Duncan: A Friday.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: So Yeah and I&#x27;m. I have to get a general medicine. I could be dying by the way, right? Um, and I&#x27;ll be going into the West End on the Saturday. Do you want me to do the Saturday right? Um, The twenty fifth to start just to give us a little like because I am out that weekend anyway, with Drew going for dinner and stuff and is football, but I can do the morning right.</p><p>Bob Duncan: So we say this twenty fifth that&#x27;ll be the first one yeah.</p><p>Karen Lochhead: Yeah. And then we can do who&#x27;s coming to visit me on the twenty fifth. Nobody, And then we can maybe do not the long weekend because I don&#x27;t want to work the long weekend in 